{
  "summary": "The 12-round hard cap with binary questions ('are new points emerging?' and 'are positions converging?') is a meaningful improvement over raw subjective verdicts, but several weaknesses remain. First, the binary questions are still LLM-judged — asking 'are new points emerging?' requires the model to decide what counts as 'new,' which is the same subjectivity problem repackaged into yes/no form rather than a three-way verdict. Binary framing reduces but does not eliminate optimism bias; a helpful model can still answer 'yes, new points' to any minor rephrasing. Second, the four-state matrix (progress, healthy debate, consensus, stall) sounds clean but 'healthy debate' is a dangerous state — it means no convergence AND new points keep appearing, which in practice could sustain indefinitely and burn through the full 12-round cap every time on any non-trivial topic. There's no proposed mechanism for distinguishing genuinely productive healthy debate from an LLM's tendency to endlessly generate novel-sounding reformulations. Third, the 'novelty ratio' safeguard mentioned as a structural check is underspecified — what exactly is being compared, at what granularity, and how do you avoid the same semantic-similarity-is-still-LLM-judgment trap that round 4 of the previous discussion already identified? Fourth, the user's suggestion of 12/16/24 rounds deserves more direct engagement: at 12 rounds with 3-4 round evaluation cycles, you get at most 3-4 evaluation checkpoints, which is reasonable, but at 24 rounds you could have 6-8 checkpoints and the cost of a full run becomes significant — the proposal doesn't address how the cap should scale with topic complexity or whether the evaluation frequency should adapt. A more robust approach might be to make the first evaluation checkpoint earlier (after round 2) with a higher bar for continuation, then progressively raise the bar at each subsequent checkpoint — this front-loads the 'is this going anywhere?' decision and naturally limits cost for topics that converge quickly while still allowing extended deliberation for complex ones."
